---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import MarkupCalculator from '../../components/MarkupCalculator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Markup Calculator" description="Calculate selling price and profit from a cost and markup percentage.">
  <MarkupCalculator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why markup and margin aren't the same number</h2>
  <p>Markup is calculated as a percentage of cost, while margin is calculated as a percentage of selling price — the same numbers, viewed differently, always produce a markup percentage higher than the equivalent margin percentage, which frequently confuses pricing conversations.</p>

  <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your cost.</li>
    <li>Enter your desired markup percentage.</li>
    <li>See the resulting selling price, profit, and equivalent margin.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A ₹100 cost with a 40% markup produces a ₹140 selling price and ₹40 profit — which works out to a 28.6% margin, not 40%, illustrating the markup-vs-margin distinction concretely.</p>

  <h2>Common Markup Calculation Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Treating a target margin percentage as if it were a markup percentage</strong> (or vice versa) when setting prices — this consistently under- or over-prices depending on the direction of the mistake.</li>
    <li><strong>Not accounting for other costs</strong> (shipping, payment processing fees) beyond the base product cost when calculating actual profit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Markup Calculator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Setting a selling price from a known cost and target markup</li>
    <li>Understanding the actual margin behind a chosen markup percentage</li>
    <li>Comparing pricing strategies expressed in markup versus margin terms</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Markup Calculator FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Which should I use, markup or margin?</strong> Markup is often more intuitive for pricing decisions ("I want 40% on top of cost"), while margin is more useful for profitability analysis as a percentage of revenue.</p>
</ToolLayout>
